"""Days3–4: identity, boundaries and a usable browser client."""
L(2,'1. Register without storing a plain password','Follow canonical identity, password hashing and duplicate handling.',
 [('Password hash verification','Store a slow password hash, then use its matching verification function. The hash is not a value to show in a response or log.'),('Canonical username','The server trims and lowercases supported ASCII usernames so duplicate identity checks have a consistent meaning.')],
 'Registration validates the account contract and hashes the secret before inserting the user. A unique database constraint protects identity even if two requests race. A duplicate returns409 with a generic useful message. The public response exposes id and username, not a hash or the original password.\n\nTreat the browser’s length hints as help, not the rule’s authority. Requests can bypass HTML entirely. The server repeats the actual contract and strips secret input values from validation responses. This project deliberately has no password-recovery flow; use fictional accounts, and document what a forgotten practice passphrase means rather than pretending recovery exists.',
 'from lesson_support import practice,PRACTICE_PASSWORD\nfrom sqlalchemy import select\nfrom werkzeug.security import check_password_hash\nfrom orm_api.models import User\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    with app.state.sessions() as db:\n        stored=db.scalar(select(User.password_hash).where(User.username=="asha"))\n        print("Plain password stored:",stored==PRACTICE_PASSWORD)\n        print("Verifies:",check_password_hash(stored,PRACTICE_PASSWORD))',
 'Verify that a repeated supported username does not create a second account.',
 'from lesson_support import practice,PRACTICE_PASSWORD\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.post("/v1/users",json={"username":"ASHA","password":PRACTICE_PASSWORD})\n    print("Conflict:",r.status_code)\n    print("Useful message:",r.json()["detail"])',
 'Inspect the public registration shape for a fresh fictional account.',
 'from lesson_support import practice,PRACTICE_PASSWORD\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.post("/v1/users",json={"username":"learner","password":PRACTICE_PASSWORD})\n    print("Public fields:",sorted(r.json()))',
 [('What belongs in the password storage column?','A suitable password hash','The plain password','The bearer token','Hash and verify using the password-hashing scheme.'),('Can HTML validation replace server validation?','No','Yes','Only on the same origin','Clients can send arbitrary HTTP requests.')],
 'Return the stored user object with every internal column.','Use explicit public response fields and never echo password material.','Identity handling combines validation, storage constraints and controlled output.')

L(2,'2. Sign in and understand the session boundary','Separate a signed token, verified claims and a revocable login record.',
 [('Bearer session','Possession of the token authorises requests within its accepted scope and expiry. This UI keeps it only in tab memory; saved notes remain server-side.'),('Verified claims and revocation','The API checks signature, algorithm, issuer, audience, required claim types and an unexpired stored login. A signature alone does not prove the session is still allowed.')],
 'The login route receives form-encoded username and password, verifies credentials and creates an individual session identifier. The response includes a signed access token with a fifteen-minute lifetime. Later requests send it in Authorization, not in a URL query. The server also checks the session row so a logout can revoke that single login.\n\nThe example decodes using the actual temporary app key, expected issuer and audience; it never prints the token or key. Reading unverified JSON is not authentication. Memory-only storage reduces persistent credential exposure but makes refresh require sign-in and does not prevent malicious scripts from abusing an active page. Combine this choice with safe rendering and server boundaries.',
 'import jwt\nfrom lesson_support import practice\nfrom orm_api import ISSUER,AUDIENCE\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    token=h["Authorization"].removeprefix("Bearer ")\n    claims=jwt.decode(token,app.state.key,algorithms=["HS256"],issuer=ISSUER,audience=AUDIENCE)\n    print("Lifetime:",claims["exp"]-claims["iat"])\n    print("Session id present:",bool(claims["jti"]))',
 'Verify a signed-in identity without inspecting secret values.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    print("Me:",c.get("/v1/me",headers=h).json()["username"])',
 'Compare a missing token with an invalid token.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    print("Missing:",c.get("/v1/me").status_code)\n    print("Invalid:",c.get("/v1/me",headers={"Authorization":"Bearer fictional-invalid"}).status_code)',
 [('Does decoding token JSON without verification authenticate a user?','No','Yes','Only with an expiry field','Trust requires the complete verification policy.'),('Where does this dashboard keep the token?','Tab memory','A committed JavaScript constant','Study Hub backups','Refresh intentionally clears the login credential.')],
 'Put a bearer token in a shareable URL and trust its decoded username.','Send the credential in the header and verify the complete session policy.','A convenient client must preserve the server’s identity boundary.')

L(2,'3. Prove authorisation with two accounts','Apply ownership to collection, detail, mutation and relationship operations.',
 [('Authentication versus authorisation','Authentication establishes the caller. Authorisation decides which resources that caller may access. A successful login does not grant every note.'),('Consistent not-found boundary','The project uses404 for another owner’s note and for a missing note. This avoids offering a separate resource-existence signal through those routes.')],
 'Run the Asha and Ben story before adding cosmetic polish. Asha creates a note. Ben’s collection stays empty, his direct read and delete return404, and his attempt to change its tags is also denied. Returning an owned collection is not enough if detail or relationship routes forget the predicate.\n\nThe server determines the principal from the verified session. It then scopes each resource query by both id and owner_id. Do not expose all notes and ask the browser to filter them. Do not accept an owner change simply because the editor has no visible control for it. The tests deliberately bypass the UI to prove those boundaries.',
 'from lesson_support import practice,login\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    other=login(c,"ben")\n    print("Other collection:",c.get("/v1/notes",headers=other).json()["total"])\n    print("Other read:",c.get("/v1/notes/1",headers=other).status_code)',
 'Try the relationship route as the other owner.',
 'from lesson_support import practice,login\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.put("/v1/notes/1/tags",headers=login(c,"ben"),json={"names":["api"]})\n    print("Tag change denied:",r.status_code)',
 'Prove a rejected foreign delete leaves the owner’s note available.',
 'from lesson_support import practice,login\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    print("Foreign delete:",c.delete("/v1/notes/1",headers=login(c,"ben")).status_code)\n    print("Owner read:",c.get("/v1/notes/1",headers=h).status_code)',
 [('Is hiding the Delete button an authorisation boundary?','No','Yes','Only on mobile','The server must reject a direct HTTP attempt.'),('Which routes need ownership checks?','Every owned resource operation','Only collection listing','Only DELETE','Detail and relationship routes need the same boundary.')],
 'Protect only the list route and rely on hard-to-guess ids elsewhere.','Scope every owned resource query to the verified caller.','An identifier is a locator, not permission.')

L(2,'4. Validate every writable boundary','Reject wrong shapes and preserve the original data on invalid changes.',
 [('Strict input contract','Extra fields, wrong JSON types, blank text, excessive lengths and forbidden nulls are rejected before applying writes.'),('Boundary-value check','Test values at and around a limit, rather than only one ordinary value. Include both acceptance and unchanged-state evidence on rejection.')],
 'The note contract requires meaningful title/body text and a real boolean. A string saying false is not the JSON value false. Patch additionally distinguishes omission from an explicit null and rejects an empty change. Search/page parameters have their own limits to avoid treating an unbounded browser request as normal.\n\nShow a clear field hint, but keep the server contract authoritative. For a failure, check more than422: retrieve the original and verify it was preserved. Do not echo invalid submitted secrets in errors. These small rules make the app predictable when users paste spaces, double-click, use an old client or send deliberately malformed input.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    for change in [{"completed":"false"},{"body":"   "},{"title":None}]:\n        print("Rejected:",c.patch("/v1/notes/1",headers=h,json=change).status_code)',
 'Test the title boundary on both sides.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    for size in [100,101]:\n        r=c.post("/v1/notes",headers=h,json={"title":"x"*size,"body":"Valid"})\n        print(size,r.status_code)',
 'Verify a malformed completion query is rejected without writing anything.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    print("Invalid filter:",c.get("/v1/notes?completed=banana",headers=h).status_code)\n    print("Still stored:",c.get("/v1/notes",headers=h).json()["total"])',
 [('Is the JSON string "false" the same as false in a strict model?','No','Yes','Only in PATCH','Type and value are separate parts of the contract.'),('What else should an invalid-write test check?','Original data remains unchanged','Only button colour','Only response timing','A rejection must preserve the stored invariant.')],
 'Coerce arbitrary values silently and rely on the browser to send good JSON.','Validate the server contract and test its boundaries.','Predictable rejection is part of a usable API.')

L(2,'5. Design errors a client can recover from','Expose safe statuses and request correlation without leaking inputs.',
 [('Error contract','A meaningful status and controlled response shape. The client maps it to a useful action instead of displaying a raw exception.'),('Request correlation id','An identifier connecting a response with redacted server telemetry. It helps diagnose a failure without logging passwords, bearer values or note content.')],
 'Different failures need different next actions.401 means the session or credentials are not accepted;404 means this note is unavailable;409 covers a conflicting username;422 identifies an invalid request. A network failure has no reliable HTTP status at all. The browser handles these categories explicitly and keeps an ordinary failed-write draft.\n\nUnexpected server exceptions produce a generic public response with a request id. Telemetry records method, route template, status and duration, not request bodies or credentials. A useful error system still needs real operational ownership when deployed. A request id is not a substitute for monitoring or a promise that every request succeeded.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.get("/v1/notes/999",headers=h)\n    print("Status:",r.status_code)\n    print("Message:",r.json()["detail"])\n    print("Correlation header:",bool(r.headers.get("x-request-id")))',
 'Check that invalid account input does not echo the submitted password.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    secret="fictional-too-short"\n    r=c.post("/v1/users",json={"username":"!","password":secret})\n    print("Invalid:",r.status_code)\n    print("Secret echoed:",secret in r.text)',
 'Confirm health response correlation is present without printing its random value.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.get("/health")\n    print("Has request id:",len(r.headers["x-request-id"])>0)',
 [('Should a public error include a raw traceback and password input?','No','Yes','Only on a small app','Provide safe feedback and redacted diagnostic correlation.'),('Does a disconnected request always have an HTTP status?','No','Always','Only on localhost','Transport failure and an HTTP error response differ.')],
 'Show the raw exception to make every failure look informative.','Map controlled failures to useful actions and keep secret inputs out.','Error handling should improve recovery without exposing private state.')

L(2,'6. Finish the session and security checklist','Verify logout revocation and state the remaining product limits.',
 [('Individual session revocation','The logout route removes the current stored login; replaying that token fails while another valid session can remain active.'),('Layered controls','Ownership, HTTPS, validation, safe rendering, secret handling and abuse controls address different risks. No one checkbox makes the app fully production-ready.')],
 'Signing out successfully calls the server revocation route and then forgets the tab credential and private workspace. Merely clearing a JavaScript variable would not revoke a copied token. If the request fails, the UI still clears this tab but warns that server revocation was not confirmed; expiry remains a separate limit.\n\nRead the public-use limits before real personal use. There is no password recovery, login rate limiting, refresh-token flow or simultaneous-edit conflict detection in this learning scope. Public HTTPS and deployment settings also need platform verification. Learn those boundaries explicitly rather than upgrading the word “demo” to “secure product” after one successful login.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    print("Before:",c.get("/v1/me",headers=h).status_code)\n    print("Logout:",c.post("/v1/logout",headers=h).status_code)\n    print("Replay:",c.get("/v1/me",headers=h).status_code)',
 'Check that revoking one login does not require destroying the account.',
 'from lesson_support import practice,login\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    other_session=login(c)\n    c.post("/v1/logout",headers=h)\n    print("Other valid session:",c.get("/v1/me",headers=other_session).status_code)',
 'List explicitly deferred identity features from the specification.',
 'import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\ns=json.loads(Path("spec.json").read_text())\nprint([v for v in s["out_of_scope"] if v in {"password recovery","refresh tokens"}])',
 [('Does clearing the browser variable alone revoke a copied token?','No','Yes','Only after refresh','Server revocation and local forgetting are distinct.'),('Does HTTPS replace note ownership checks?','No','Always','Only on a managed host','Transport protection and authorisation solve different problems.')],
 'Call logout complete after hiding the username while the server session remains valid.','Revoke the session, clear local private state and explain unconfirmed remote logout.','A clear session lifecycle is both security and user experience.')

L(3,'1. Serve a simple frontend on the same origin','Trace HTML, CSS and JavaScript from the Python server to the API.',
 [('Origin','A scheme, hostname and port combination. Relative fetch URLs keep this client on the same origin as its API.'),('Static assets versus application data','HTML/CSS/JavaScript describe the interface. Private notes arrive through authenticated API calls, not hard-coded source or a frontend build secret.')],
 'Open the app through python app.py, then visit the loopback URL. The root route serves dashboard.html, which loads local dashboard.css and dashboard.js. The API lives on that same host and port, so the client can fetch relative /v1 paths without adding a cross-origin architecture. The optional explorer remains at /docs.\n\nThe syntax box below is browser HTML, not Python. Use the supplied static files while the Python example verifies the real server response. No external fonts, analytics or script packages are needed by this dashboard. That does not make server writes available without the server: an offline learning page and a database-backed app have different runtime needs.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    page=c.get("/")\n    print("HTML status:",page.status_code)\n    print("Local script:","/assets/dashboard.js" in page.text)\n    print("Local stylesheet:","/assets/dashboard.css" in page.text)',
 'Confirm both local frontend assets can be retrieved.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    for path in ["/assets/dashboard.js","/assets/dashboard.css"]:\n        print(path,c.get(path).status_code)',
 'Describe which layer owns each state.',
 'owner={"page layout":"static frontend","saved notes":"server database","current editor":"tab memory"}\nfor name,layer in owner.items():print(name+":",layer)',
 [('Can opening dashboard.html as a file run the Python routes?','No','Yes','Only on Windows','Use the running server for the full-stack workflow.'),('Where do saved private notes belong?','Authenticated server storage','A public script constant','A GitHub Pages source file','Frontend source is not a private database.')],
 'Open the HTML directly and assume relative API URLs now execute Python.','Run the app and load its same-origin dashboard.','The browser UI is one client of the running application.',syntax='<!-- Browser HTML in dashboard.html -->\n<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/dashboard.css">\n<script src="/assets/dashboard.js" defer></script>')

L(3,'2. Build forms and a small explicit client state','Give each input a label and separate draft state from saved state.',
 [('DOM; labelled form','The browser’s document object model exposes controls and events. A label connected to an input helps people and assistive technology understand its purpose.'),('Client state and submit event','The closure stores the current token, editor id, filters and page. preventDefault lets the handler send the intended API request rather than navigating away with a normal form submission.')],
 'Read the actual authForm and noteForm elements. Required fields, maximum lengths and password input types help the learner before a request. The JavaScript still trims note text and relies on server validation. A draft in the textarea is not yet a saved note; it changes state only after the response confirms a write.\n\nThe browser syntax below belongs in dashboard.js. Use a submit handler so keyboard submission and button submission share one path. The mutation guard disables controls and rejects a second action while a write is in flight. This prevents an ordinary duplicate click, but it does not create server-side idempotency for a request whose response was lost.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    html=c.get("/").text\n    print("Editor form:",\'id="noteForm"\' in html)\n    print("Label linked:",\'for="noteTitle"\' in html)\n    print("Title control:",\'id="noteTitle"\' in html)',
 'Confirm that the server still rejects a blank form payload.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    print("Server rejects spaces:",c.post("/v1/notes",headers=h,json={"title":" ","body":" "}).status_code)',
 'Separate local draft and confirmed save in a small state sketch.',
 'state={"draft":"A thought","saved_id":None}\nprint("Saved:",state["saved_id"] is not None)\nstate["saved_id"]=7\nprint("Saved after confirmation:",state["saved_id"] is not None)',
 [('Does a textarea value prove a committed save?','No','Yes','Only after typing stops','The server response establishes the save boundary.'),('Does a disabled button implement server idempotency?','No','Always','Only for POST','It stops ordinary duplicate UI submissions, not uncertain remote writes.')],
 'Handle only mouse clicks and call the draft saved before the request finishes.','Use form submission and explicit confirmed state.','Accessible interaction and honest saved state share the same event flow.',syntax='// Browser JavaScript\nnoteForm.addEventListener("submit", event => {\n  event.preventDefault();\n  // validate draft, await API, then show confirmed result\n});')

L(3,'3. Make fetch handle success, HTTP errors and transport errors','Read the response contract instead of treating every resolved promise as success.',
 [('fetch; Promise; async / await','fetch starts an HTTP request and returns a Promise. await waits for its result inside an async function; the UI can remain responsive.'),('response.ok; empty204; timeout','HTTP4xx/5xx responses normally resolve fetch, so check ok. A204 has no JSON body. A rejected or timed-out request is a transport outcome, not a known server status.')],
 'Read api() in dashboard.js before writing individual button handlers. It prepares headers and the appropriate JSON or form body, applies a bounded timeout, checks response.ok and handles204 without parsing JSON. It maps expected statuses to controlled messages and treats session expiry as a private-state boundary.\n\nDo not display an arbitrary HTML error body as trusted markup. Do not automatically repeat every failed POST: if the server committed but the response disappeared, the write outcome is uncertain. The project preserves ordinary failed-write drafts, but it does not claim an idempotency-key implementation. This Python example verifies the relevant server contracts; the real browser suite verifies the request helper’s behaviour.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    print("Read success:",c.get("/v1/notes",headers=h).is_success)\n    print("Invalid HTTP response:",c.patch("/v1/notes/1",headers=h,json={}).status_code)\n    deleted=c.delete("/v1/notes/1",headers=h)\n    print("Empty successful body:",deleted.content==b"")',
 'Map HTTP categories to useful next actions.',
 'actions={401:"sign in again",404:"refresh unavailable note",422:"fix request values",503:"retry after checking service"}\nfor status,action in actions.items():print(status,action)',
 'Recognise that missing confirmation is a distinct outcome.',
 'outcome={"request_sent":True,"confirmation_received":False}\nprint("Can assert committed:",outcome["confirmation_received"])\nprint("Inspect before blindly retrying:",not outcome["confirmation_received"])',
 [('Does fetch reject merely because the server returned422?','No','Always','Only for POST','Check the HTTP response status explicitly.'),('Should a204 response always be parsed as JSON?','No','Yes','Only after DELETE','It intentionally has an empty response body.')],
 'Assume await fetch means success and always call response.json().','Check ok, handle204 and distinguish transport uncertainty.','A shared request helper makes every workflow follow the same contract.',syntax='// Browser JavaScript pattern\nconst response = await fetch("/v1/notes", options);\nif (!response.ok) throw new Error("Request was not accepted");\nreturn response.status === 204 ? null : response.json();')

L(3,'4. Render user text without executing it','Use DOM text nodes and keep private credentials out of persistent browser storage.',
 [('textContent; createElement','createElement makes a DOM node. textContent inserts text rather than parsing it as HTML; notes remain ordinary learner-authored strings.'),('Stored script injection boundary','A string saved in the database can still attack a page if later interpreted as executable markup. Safe insertion belongs at the rendering boundary.')],
 'A note may contain angle brackets because someone is studying HTML or because they are probing the renderer. Store the intended plain text under the server contract, then render it with textContent. The dashboard creates each card and button through DOM methods; it does not concatenate a note title into innerHTML.\n\nThe server preserving a string is not evidence that the browser handles it safely. The Python example below confirms preservation only. The real browser test separately saves an injection-shaped body and confirms it did not execute. Memory-only tokens are not a substitute for safe rendering: malicious same-page code could still act with a current session.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    text="<img src=x onerror=fictional()>"\n    r=c.post("/v1/notes",headers=h,json={"title":"Markup study","body":text})\n    print("Text preserved:",r.json()["body"]==text)\n    print("Browser execution tested here:",False)',
 'Check the shipped rendering mechanism without claiming that source inspection is a browser test.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ns=Path("orm_api/static/dashboard.js").read_text()\nprint("Text insertion used:","node.textContent=text" in s)\nprint("Markup insertion used:","innerHTML" in s)',
 'List frontend states that should not be copied into course backups.',
 'private=["bearer credential","password input","temporary session"]\nprint("Keep out of public source:",", ".join(private))',
 [('Which insertion fits plain learner notes?','textContent','Unescaped innerHTML','eval(note.body)','Treat learner strings as data, not executable code.'),('Does a server text-preservation test prove browser non-execution?','No','Yes','Only on localhost','Verify the rendering boundary in an actual browser.')],
 'Use innerHTML because the text came back from your own database.','Create nodes and assign learner strings through textContent.','Stored data is still untrusted at the browser interpretation boundary.',syntax='// Browser JavaScript\nconst paragraph = document.createElement("p");\nparagraph.textContent = note.body;\ncard.append(paragraph);')

L(3,'5. Make an interrupted Save recoverable','Retain the saved note identity when the later tag request fails.',
 [('Partial-save state','The client records which part the server confirmed. After a note write succeeds, editing becomes that note id even if its tag update fails.'),('Recoverable retry versus atomicity','A retry that updates the known resource avoids a duplicate after confirmed partial success. It does not turn two independent requests into one database transaction.')],
 'Follow noteForm.onsubmit in the supplied source line by line. Validate the draft, await POST or PATCH, retain the returned id, then update tag links. Only after both confirmations does the editor clear. If tags fail, the saved note is visible and the editor remains attached to it with a specific message.\n\nThere is another boundary: losing the first write’s response leaves no confirmed id. This sample does not solve that with an idempotency key. Inspect/refetch before retrying an uncertain create. Likewise, a later read failure can occur after both writes succeeded; preserve the known save facts rather than claiming the write was rolled back. Learn to describe those stages accurately.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.post("/v1/notes",headers=h,json={"title":"Partial example","body":"Saved first"})\n    url=r.headers["Location"]\n    print("Note saved:",r.status_code)\n    print("Invalid tags:",c.put(url+"/tags",headers=h,json={"names":["unknown"]}).status_code)\n    print("Note remains:",c.get(url,headers=h).status_code)',
 'Retry the second stage on the confirmed note instead of creating another.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.post("/v1/notes",headers=h,json={"title":"One identity","body":"Fixture"})\n    print("Tag retry:",c.put(r.headers["Location"]+"/tags",headers=h,json={"names":["api"]}).status_code)\n    print("Total after retry:",c.get("/v1/notes",headers=h).json()["total"])',
 'Write the minimal state needed for a confirmed partial retry.',
 'editor={"saved_note_id":12,"tags_confirmed":False}\nprint("Next action:","update tags" if editor["saved_note_id"] else "create note")',
 [('What is confirmed when POST succeeds but PUT tags fails?','The note exists; tag change is unconfirmed','Nothing could have saved','The entire operation was atomic','Respect each request’s transaction boundary.'),('Does the project solve a lost first POST response with idempotency keys?','No','Yes','Only for tags','That uncertain-write capability remains outside this sample.')],
 'Clear the editor on the first response or blindly create again after every failure.','Track confirmed stages and retry the known resource appropriately.','Recovery depends on knowing what actually crossed the save boundary.',syntax='// Browser JavaScript sequence\nconst note = await api(path, writeOptions);\nstate.editing = note.id;\nawait api(`/v1/notes/${note.id}/tags`, tagOptions);\nresetEditor();')

L(3,'6. Keep filters, pages and asynchronous reads consistent','Prevent an old response from replacing a newer session or search result.',
 [('Request generation counter','Each list request receives a newer ticket. A result is applied only if its ticket and session token still match current state.'),('Last-page recovery','After filtering or deleting, calculate the valid last offset. If the current offset exceeds it, load that valid page instead of leaving a false empty page.')],
 'HTTP results can arrive out of order. A slow search started before logout may finish afterward. The client increments readVersion when starting a read and when clearing the session, then checks both ticket and token before rendering. The check protects the state application boundary; it does not undo a server operation.\n\nChanging filters resets offset. Deleting the last row on the last page triggers a valid earlier page. Count and items always come from the same server query. The source also captures the request token before sending, so an old401 does not clear a different newer login. The browser suite deliberately delays a real list response across logout to verify the private UI stays empty.',
 'current_version=4;request_ticket=3\nprint("Apply old result:",request_ticket==current_version)\nrequest_ticket=4\nprint("Apply current result:",request_ticket==current_version)',
 'Calculate the valid last offset for five-row pages.',
 'limit=5\nfor total in [0,1,5,6,10]:\n    last=max(0,((total-1)//limit)*limit)\n    print(total,last)',
 'Use the real API to observe an offset beyond the final page.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    p=c.get("/v1/notes?limit=5&offset=20",headers=h).json()\n    print("Total:",p["total"])\n    print("Rows at old offset:",len(p["items"]))',
 [('Should a slow pre-logout response repopulate private cards?','No','Yes','Only if200','Apply results only to the matching current state.'),('What should happen to offset when changing filters?','Reset it','Keep every old offset','Multiply it by total','The previous page position may not exist in the new collection.')],
 'Render every response in arrival order without checking the current session.','Validate request generation and identity before applying read results.','Asynchronous correctness is part of a trustworthy interface.',syntax='// Browser JavaScript pattern\nconst ticket = ++state.readVersion;\nconst token = state.token;\nconst page = await api(url);\nif (ticket !== state.readVersion || token !== state.token) return;')
